[17592186492699 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa0732c4-d78b-5f63-bac2-1d79627fdaad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492812 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f440a145-0bf3-5ff0-b0c3-e81bf8ba0f0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb1325f4-cc8e-5bd1-b83f-e768c4904fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,∗\\, & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20edcd99-4d6d-5145-9c47-d9e4447f46d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second and third columns of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e34d3f6-344a-52de-9e80-5526338f9855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the second and third columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186505899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505094 #edb/ref 1044 